{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bae689bb3c65ffe","type":"tool_result","content":"# Component: mlflow\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/mlflow.git\n- **Version**: 1cccfac973b98eea1cafe03565da6a47ea08c449\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Python\n- **Deployment Type**: Python SDK\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: MLflow Tracking Server providing experiment tracking, model registry, and artifact serving with Kubernetes-native authentication and workspace isolation for RHOAI. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:80, mlflow/server/__init__.py:218-226, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:1-11]\n\n**Detailed**: MLflow is deployed as a containerized tracking server on port 5000, serving the MLflow UI, REST API, and artifact proxy behind the `kubernetes-auth` authentication plugin from the external `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` package (v1.5.0). The server runs Python 3.12 under gunicorn/uvicorn on a UBI9-minimal base image, with Kubernetes service account bearer tokens providing authentication and the `mlflow/server/auth` module enforcing RBAC-based authorization with roles, permissions, and per-resource access control. The `--enable-workspaces` flag activates multi-tenant workspace isolation backed by a Kubernetes workspace store (`kubernetes://`), where each workspace maps to a Kubernetes namespace. The component surface includes 6 architecture components—the container entrypoint, gunicorn and uvicorn ASGI/WSGI servers, the mlflow and mlflow-skinny Python services, and the mlflow-tracing SDK—exposing 48 HTTP endpoints for experiment tracking, model registry, gateway proxy, assistant, job execution, and MCP server APIs. External integrations include S3-compatible storage via boto3, Google Cloud Storage, and the Kubernetes API for project execution as Jobs. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:49-58, 76-80, requirements/konflux-pypi.in:13, mlflow/server/__init__.py:218-226, mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:203-258, mlflow/projects/kubernetes.py:8-9, 32-49]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nMLflow operates in RHOAI as a multi-tenant experiment tracking and model registry server. The container entrypoint (`Dockerfile.konflux:80`) starts the MLflow tracking server with three key flags that define its RHOAI-specific behavior: `--app-name kubernetes-auth` loads the server-side authentication plugin from the separately packaged `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` (v1.5.0, installed from PyPI via hermetic Konflux build), `--enable-workspaces` activates namespace-scoped workspace isolation, and `--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` delegates workspace storage to the Kubernetes API. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:80, requirements/konflux-pypi.in:13]\n\nThe authentication architecture has two layers. On the server side, the `--app-name kubernetes-auth` entry point loads an auth middleware plugin that intercepts API requests. On the client side, `mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py` provides two auth providers activated via `MLFLOW_TRACKING_AUTH`: `kubernetes` (bearer token only) and `kubernetes-namespaced` (bearer token plus `X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE` header derived from the pod's service account namespace). Both resolve credentials from the Kubernetes service account token file at `/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token`, falling back to kubeconfig when running outside a cluster. The core `mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py` module provides RBAC enforcement with role-based permissions, per-resource access control for experiments, registered models, gateway endpoints, and workspaces. [source: mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:1-11, 38-41, 203-294, mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/server/__init__.py:218-226]\n\nThe Kubernetes API integration serves two distinct purposes: project execution (creating and monitoring Kubernetes Jobs for ML experiment runs via `mlflow/projects/kubernetes.py`) and workspace management (the `kubernetes://` workspace store URI). The project execution path uses the `kubernetes` Python client to construct Job manifests, push container images, and poll Job status. [source: mlflow/projects/kubernetes.py:8-9, 32-49, Dockerfile.konflux:80]\n\nThe RHOAI container disables several upstream features via environment variables: `MLFLOW_ENABLE_AI_GATEWAY=false`, `MLFLOW_ENABLE_ASSISTANT=false`, `MLFLOW_SERVER_ENABLE_JOB_EXECUTION=false`, and `MLFLOW_DISABLE_TELEMETRY=true`. This means the gateway and assistant HTTP endpoints present in the codebase are not active in the default RHOAI deployment. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:38-43]\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage\n\n| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n| Upstream | https://github.com/mlflow/mlflow | -- | -- | -- | known_mapping |\n| Midstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/mlflow | manual | main | -- | sync_config |\n| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/mlflow | manual | main | `check-operator-workflow-sync.yml`, `version-sync.yml` | local_analysis |\n\n### Aliases\n\n| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n|------------|-------------|----|-------|\n\n## Architecture Components\n\n| Component | Type | Purpose |\n|---------|----|-------|\n| Dockerfile.konflux:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"python3.12\", \"-m\", \"mlflow\", \"server\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"5000\", \"--app-name\", \"kubernetes-auth\", \"--enable-workspaces\", \"--workspace-store-uri\", \"kubernetes://\", \"--serve-artifacts\"] |\n| gunicorn | Python ASGI/WSGI server | Python entrypoint |\n| mlflow | Python Service (FastAPI), Python console script | MLflow is an open source platform for the complete machine learning lifecycle; mlflow.cli:cli |\n| mlflow-skinny | Python Service (FastAPI) | MLflow is an open source platform for the complete machine learning lifecycle |\n----\n# Component: mlflow-operator\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator.git\n- **Version**: 8a8c12a2a3df1349b1bd5424c42f4214b8206d62\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Go\n- **Deployment Type**: Kubernetes Operator / Controller\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of MLflow Tracking Server instances on OpenShift, including Helm-based deployment, Gateway API routing, namespace-scoped workspace RBAC, and integration with the RHOAI platform Auth service. [source: cmd/main.go:171, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:539, internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:67]\n\n**Detailed**: mlflow-operator is a controller-runtime operator that reconciles three custom resources — `MLflow` (mlflow.opendatahub.io/v1), `MLflowOperator` (components.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1), and `MLflowConfig` (mlflow.kubeflow.org/v1) — to deploy and manage MLflow Tracking Server instances on RHOAI. The MLflow controller renders Helm charts into Kubernetes resources (Deployments, Services, PersistentVolumeClaims, CronJobs, NetworkPolicies) and conditionally creates Gateway API HTTPRoutes, OpenShift ConsoleLinks, and Prometheus ServiceMonitors based on cluster capability discovery. A dedicated NamespaceRBACReconciler watches the platform Auth CRD (`services.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1`) and labeled namespaces to propagate view/edit RoleBindings for workspace-level access control. The operator uses leader election, caches filtered by label selectors, and field-selector-scoped watches for shared RBAC objects to minimize API server load. [source: cmd/main.go:171, 272, 300, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:473-552, internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:48-106, internal/controller/routing.go:46-179]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nmlflow-operator follows a multi-controller architecture built on controller-runtime v0.23.3. The primary **MLflowReconciler** owns the full lifecycle of MLflow Tracking Server deployments: it renders bundled Helm charts into Kubernetes objects (Deployments, Services, Secrets, ServiceAccounts, PersistentVolumeClaims, CronJobs, and NetworkPolicies) and applies them via server-side apply with owner references. The controller conditionally extends its watch surface at startup using the Kubernetes discovery API — adding Gateway API HTTPRoute, OpenShift ConsoleLink, and Prometheus ServiceMonitor watches only when those CRDs are present in the cluster. This discovery-gated pattern avoids hard dependencies on optional platform APIs. [source: internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:528-552, internal/controller/routing.go:46-179]\n\nThe **MLflowOperatorReconciler** manages the component-level `MLflowOperator` CRD from `components.platform.opendatahub.io`, acting as the integration surface for the RHOAI platform operator (odh-operator). A dedicated **NamespaceRBACReconciler** watches namespaces labeled with `opendatahub.io/mlflow-workspace`, the platform Auth CRD (`services.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1`), and MLflow CRs to propagate view/edit RoleBindings into workspace namespaces, enabling multi-tenant access isolation delegated through the platform's centralized auth configuration. [source: internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:48-106, internal/controller/mlflowoperator_controller.go:76-143]\n\nThe operator restricts its Kubernetes API footprint through label-selector-filtered caches (keyed on `app=mlflow`) and field-selector-scoped watches for shared RBAC objects (ClusterRole/ClusterRoleBinding filtered by `metadata.name`). Secret access is restricted via `resourceNames`-scoped RBAC to the single `mlflow-artifact-connection` secret per namespace. The metrics endpoint on port 8443 is protected by controller-runtime's TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview filter with TLS (self-signed or operator-provided certificates), while health and readiness probes on port 8081 are unauthenticated by design. FIPS compliance is enforced at build time via `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` and dynamic linking against OpenSSL on UBI9. [source: cmd/main.go:267-284, 293-318, Dockerfile.konflux:31-32, config/rbac/role.yaml:2]\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage\n\n| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n| Upstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/mlflow-operator | manual | main | -- | sync_config |\n| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator | manual | main | -- | local_analysis |\n\n### Aliases\n\n| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n|------------|-------------|----|-------|\n\n## Architecture Components\n\n| Component | Type | Purpose |\n|---------|----|-------|\n| Dockerfile.konflux:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| MLflow controller | Controller | Reconciles MLflow resources |\n| MLflowOperator controller | Controller | Reconciles MLflowOperator resources |\n| Namespace controller | Controller | Reconciles Namespace resources |\n| cmd | Go controller-runtime operator | cmd |\n| mlflow-operator-controller-manager | Deployment | manager (controller:latest) |\n| mlflow-tests/images/Dockerfile.konflux:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"bash\", \"mlflow-tests/images/test-run.sh\"] |\n\n## APIs Exposed\n\n### Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs)\n\nCRD count scope: 3 core API CRDs; 3 total CRD/API rows including configuration and visibility APIs.\n\n| Group | Version | Kind | Scope | API Role | Purpose |\n|-----|-------|----|-----|--------|-------|","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-