{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-eecbcb60-bb79-49e9-bdce-0d4702dedd9f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8621d5da5a2bbe3d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-imnpjyjx/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n\n## Overview\n\nThe strategy makes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server (workspace-validated, fail-closed artifact serving), extends the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` auth plugin so workspace resolution works in a server context with uninitialized tracking/registry stores, and has the MLflow operator deploy a split-server topology (dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute, with artifact traffic routed from the main tracking deployment). All work is owned by the MLflow Team across three strategy components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins).\n\nTwo genuine unknowns gate implementation and become Investigation epics:\n\n1. Whether workspace store initialization / workspace resolution in `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` is coupled to full tracking server initialization (determines whether the plugin epic is verification-only or includes a refactor — `rewrite` gate).\n2. How artifact traffic is routed from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, and whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations (two bounded outcomes → conditional decomposition branch).\n\nTwo strategy open questions were evaluated against the Step 3 decision rule and classified as acceptance criteria rather than Investigations (rationale in Health Warnings): the per-request latency threshold / caching-at-launch question (resolvable only by measuring the implemented validation — post-implementation measurement, fix-and-retry within the same epic), and the audit telemetry channel question (the P2 HLR itself pins \"through server logs\"; any channel choice lands on the same denial call sites in the same epic).\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Logged overrides:\n\n| # | Staff Input | Effect on decomposition |\n|---|-------------|------------------------|\n| 1 | No new server flag — combine `--artifacts-only` with the existing `--enable-workspaces`; remove all references to `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` | The server implementation epic's scope is flag composition only; no epic introduces a new server flag or the removed CR field. The operator epic enables the split topology through an opt-in in the MLflow CR spec without prescribing the removed field. |\n| 2 | Operator work is a split deployment (dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute + artifact traffic routing), not a configuration toggle | The operator epic is scoped as the full split-server topology, not a values-propagation change. |\n| 3 | SSAR detail is over-specified — the strategy must not dictate Kubernetes API mechanics; authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure; kube-rbac-proxy and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` unchanged | The plugin epic reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without prescribing SelfSubjectAccessReview mechanics; the unchanged auth setup is recorded as a non-goal/AC. |\n\nThe strategy's Technical Approach already reflects these corrections (it describes the combined-flags and split-deployment approach with SME attributions); the decomposition follows it.\n\n## Component Mappings\n\n- `.context/rhai-components.txt` contains exactly one MLflow-related canonical component: `MLflow`. The strategy components **MLflow Operator** and **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins** are not in the canonical list, so every epic carries `component: MLflow` (the closest canonical name — `submit.py` strips unknown names). Epic boundaries still follow the strategy's three distinct components/repositories, not the collapsed canonical label.\n- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins** has no standalone file in `.context/architecture-context/`, but is documented within `mlflow.md` (external package v1.5.0, `kubernetes-auth` plugin, `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point, `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`), and the strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\". It is treated as an existing component — no repo-onboarding / konflux-onboarding / license-validation chain is generated. All three components already build via Konflux (`Dockerfile.konflux` referenced in `mlflow.md` and `mlflow-operator.md`).\n- The end-to-end validation epic spans all three strategy components; `MLflow` is assigned as the most-relevant canonical name (the capability under test is MLflow artifact serving behavior).\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Workspace resolution initialization dependency in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Independent workspace resolution path in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server MLflow topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Split-server artifact serving topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | End-to-end integration testing of workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Artifact operations delegation in the MLflow tracking server (conditional, outcome B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nMain-plan epics: 6. Conditional branch epics: 1 (`RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007`). Epic count including branches: 7.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 Investigation: plugin init dependency] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 Implementation: plugin workspace resolution]\n    E002 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 Implementation: workspace-aware artifacts-only server]\n    E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 Investigation: routing mechanism] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 Implementation: operator split topology]\n    E003 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 Implementation: end-to-end testing]\n    E005 --> E006\n    E004 -.-> BRANCHB[RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 Implementation: server-side delegation, outcome B only]\n```\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent. `E001` and `E004` run in parallel (independent unknowns, different repositories). `E003` and `E005` run in parallel (different repositories; see DAG Justification). The dashed edge gates a conditional branch epic, not a main-plan node.\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 → E006 (length 4).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The investigation's outcome determines whether the plugin epic is verification-only or includes refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — a `rewrite` true gate recorded via `gated_by`. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (library/framework → consumer serial) | The server's artifact-request validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution capability (loaded via the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`); the server work cannot be completed or tested before that capability exists in the artifacts-only context. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The selected routing mechanism (gateway-level path routing vs tracking-server delegation) changes the operator implementation's approach — a `rewrite` true gate recorded via `gated_by`. |\n| E003 → E006 | Verification ordering | The end-to-end scenario suite exercises the workspace-aware artifacts-only server capability; it cannot be authored/executed against the real behavior before that capability is implemented. |\n| E005 → E006 | Verification ordering | The split-server routing scenarios require the operator-deployed topology (dedicated Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute with artifact traffic routing). |\n| E004 → BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3 / conditional branch | The branch epic exists only if the investigation selects outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations). It is not a main-plan node; no main-plan epic depends on it. |\n| No edge E001 ∥ E004 | Rule 14 (independent, different repos) | The two investigations resolve unrelated unknowns in different repositories (plugin vs operator); no shared artifact. |\n| No edge E003 ∥ E005 | Rules 10/14 (parallel; coupling as AC, not edge) | Operator chart/reconciler work in `mlflow-operator` does not build against the server code in `mlflow` — the flag combination it renders is a configuration reference. The coupling (\"the deployed instance runs a server build supporting the combined flags\") is enforced by the end-to-end epic's acceptance criteria, which depends on both, never by a DAG edge between them. |\n| No edge E002 ∥ E004/E005 | Rule 14 | Different repositories, no shared artifacts. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving | P0 | E003 | Server flag composition, workspace-provider-only initialization. |\n| Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; non-members denied with HTTP 403 | P0 | E002, E003 (verified end-to-end in E006) | Plugin provides resolution; server enforces validation on artifact requests. |\n| Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable | P0 | E003 (verified end-to-end in E006) | Deny with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable. |\n| Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior | P0 | E003 (verified end-to-end in E006) | Backwards-compatibility ACs on the server epic; single-tenant regression in the E2E suite. |\n| MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 (verified end-to-end in E006) | |\n| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | P1 | E005, plus RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 under routing outcome B (verified end-to-end in E006) | Routing mechanism selected by E004; outcome B additionally requires server-side delegation support. |\n| Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | P1 | E002 (informed by E001) | |\n| Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | P2 | E003 **(bundled)** | Bundled, not split: the audit events are emitted from the same 403-denial call sites the P0 validation work creates in the same request-handling path — a separate epic would force a second pass over the identical surface and cannot ship independent user value. The P2 HLR itself pins the channel (\"through server logs\"); see Health Warnings for the open PM/Security telemetry-channel question. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Strategy-level process gates pending human review.** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all listed as \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. These are strategy-level sign-offs that gate the start of implementation work generally; they are not epic-scoped and are not modeled as DAG nodes.\n- **Open question — per-request latency threshold (PM/Engineering).** Classified as an acceptance criterion on the server epic (measure overhead; implement short-TTL caching per the strategy's risk mitigation if measurement shows need), not an Investigation: the question is only answerable by measuring the implemented validation, so it cannot gate the decomposition — the same epic implements, measures, and applies caching (fix-and-retry). If the PM decision lands on \"caching required at launch\", that scope is already inside the server epic; if \"defer\", the measurement AC still holds.\n- **Open question — audit telemetry channel (PM/Security).** The P2 HLR pins \"through server logs\", which is how the bundled AC on the server epic is written. If the pending PM/Security decision selects a different channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log), that is follow-up scope on the same denial call sites — recorded here so the decision is visible, not discovered later.\n- **Artifact path resolution assumption.** The strategy assumes artifact request paths carry enough information (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`) to resolve workspace membership, scoped initially to the S3 artifact store (standard RHOAI configuration). Non-standard artifact roots are documented as a limitation (AC on the server epic). If path resolution proves unworkable for the S3 layout, the server epic's approach changes — tracked as an assumption AC, not a separate Investigation, because the resolution is produced by the same implementation work (fix-and-retry).\n- **Epic kept above the ~2-week guideline — operator split topology (E005).** Effort estimate: 2-3 sprints (per the strategy's estimate). A split would duplicate work: the dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, flag propagation, and routing configuration render from shared Helm chart values and the same reconciler path — dividing them forces two passes over the same chart scaffolding and CR spec handling. Conditional re-split trigger: re-split by resource kind (instance deployment vs traffic routing) if the routing mechanism selected by the investigation requires coordination beyond operator-owned configuration, or if chart implementation exceeds the 2-3 sprint estimate.\n- **Canonical component collapse.** All epics carry `component: MLflow` because the canonical list has no entries for MLflow Operator or mlflow-kubernetes-plugins (see Component Mappings). Jira epic components will not distinguish the three repositories; the repo is identified in each epic's scope.\n- **E2E epic priority.** The end-to-end testing epic is P1: the P0 HLRs are already carried to acceptance at server level by the server epic's ACs (the strategy's proposed ACs 1-4 are testable against a directly-started server); the E2E epic additionally covers the P1 operator topology and split-server routing.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar) are internal and already exist per the strategy's dependency table; there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The only conditional structure is the routing-mechanism branch below.\n\n## Conditional Decomposition Branches\n\nGated by investigation **RHAISTRAT-8009-E004** (artifact traffic routing mechanism), two bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A — gateway-level routing.** Artifact traffic is routed at the HTTPRoute layer (path-based) from the main deployment's route to the dedicated instance; the tracking server needs no configuration change. **No branch epics** — the routing configuration is operator-owned work inside the main-plan operator epic.\n- **Outcome B — tracking-server delegation.** The tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. **Branch epic `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007`** (MLflow component) adds server-side delegation support; the operator-side wiring of that configuration remains in the main-plan operator epic (its routing approach is rewritten accordingly).\n\nBranch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":56559,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-beddc54cf64e5d56","agent_id":"a5fc108e902f0ec98","session_id":"29d6ac74-9e14-4313-a4f4-972162f9d527","uuid":"07bfb29d-0e21-442d-97b1-2a689df05848","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:43:21.444Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
