{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8bf7ba5d-c601-4d97-bf07-fdb9baac0765","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-97757c92e273ff52","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-imnpjyjx/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E001.md","content":"# Workspace resolution initialization dependency in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin\n\n## Description\n\nThis Investigation resolves whether workspace resolution in the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` auth plugin can function in an artifacts-only MLflow server context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The strategy flags this as a genuine unknown: the plugin already provides experiment-to-workspace mapping for the tracking server's non-artifact operations (registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`), but it may be coupled to the full tracking server initialization path. The outcome determines whether the plugin implementation work is verification-only or includes refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze the plugin's workspace store initialization and workspace resolution code paths to determine, with code-level references, whether they depend on tracking store or model registry store initialization.\n- Determine whether the `kubernetes://` workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without initializing tracking stores (a strategy assumption marked \"needs validation\").\n- Exercise workspace resolution in a server context with tracking/registry stores uninitialized (locally, with stubbed stores where possible; against a live Kubernetes API for peripheral verification).\n- If coupling is found: identify the specific coupling points and define the scope of an independent initialization path for workspace resolution within the plugin, sufficient to plan the plugin implementation work with no residual unknowns that would change its approach.\n- Deliverable: a findings report with a pass/fail verdict per question and, if refactoring is required, a scoped refactor definition.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The findings document states, with code references, whether workspace store initialization and workspace resolution in `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` depend on tracking store or model registry store initialization (verdict: coupled / not coupled).\n- The findings document states explicitly whether the existing workspace resolution path works unmodified in a server context where tracking and model registry stores are uninitialized, demonstrated by an executable check or reproducible test steps.\n- If coupling is found, the findings document defines the refactor scope (specific coupling points and the shape of an independent initialization path) so the plugin implementation can be planned without further design unknowns.\n- The experiment-to-workspace mapping provided by the `kubernetes://` workspace store is confirmed to be accessible without tracking store initialization, or the specific limitation is documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Covers the strategy dependency \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin (internal, exists): may require extension if workspace resolution is currently coupled to the full tracking server initialization path\" and the strategy assumption \"The workspace store (`kubernetes://` URI scheme) provides experiment-to-workspace mapping that is accessible without initializing tracking stores — needs validation\".\n- Informs (gates) the P1 HLR \"Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\" and, transitively, the P0 workspace-validation HLRs it enables.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":60177,"output_tokens":0},"context_ma