ction itself does not. |\n| human_judgment_required | 0 | Technical design decision within the MLflow Team; no stakeholder, governance, or external-party input is required. |\n# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 (Implementation)\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| change_specificity | 0 | Resource kinds (Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute), flag propagation, and validation behavior are specified, but the CR opt-in surface for the split topology is not defined in the strategy, and the routing mechanism is pending the gating investigation's outcome. |\n| pattern_precedent | 1 | The operator already renders Helm chart templates into Deployments, Services, and conditional HTTPRoutes via Server-Side Apply — adding a second rendered deployment follows the established pattern. |\n| adapter_pattern | 1 | Follows the existing Helm-in-operator reference implementation (CR spec to Helm values, embedded chart templates, Server-Side Apply) and the conditional CRD discovery pattern for optional dependencies. |\n| existing_foundation | 1 | Extends the existing operator, chart architecture, and reconciler; no new controller or structural change to the chart architecture is expected (a strategy assumption carried as an AC). |\n| open_questions | -1 | The artifact traffic routing mechanism is unresolved until the gating investigation completes, and it changes this epic's routing implementation approach. |\n| external_dependency | 0 | Gateway API is an internal, existing dependency handled by runtime CRD discovery; no upstream contribution or vendor coordination needed. |\n| human_process_gates | 0 | No approval step beyond normal code review. |\n| repo_access | 1 | The mlflow-operator repository is accessible to the team and modifiable. |\n| architecture_claims | 1 | The mlflow-operator architecture context file corroborates the premise: it documents the Helm-in-operator pattern, rendering of Deployments/Services, and conditional Gateway API HTTPR