konflux:76-80, mlflow/server/__init__.py:68-88, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:229-258]\n287:- **Runtime inventory:** The container runs gunicorn or uvicorn as the ASGI/WSGI server, hosting the Flask-based MLflow tracking server. The 6 runtime components form a layered stack: the container entrypoint invokes `mlflow server`, which initializes the Flask app with security middleware, endpoint routing, workspace handlers, and optional Prometheus metrics export. The mlflow-tracing SDK is packaged alongside but operates as a client-side instrumentation library. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:80, mlflow/server/__init__.py:68-96]\n288:- **Downstream interactions:** The tracking server stores experiment metadata in a SQL backend (PostgreSQL via psycopg2, or SQLite) and manages artifacts via a pluggable store that can target S3-compatible storage (boto3), Google Cloud Storage, or local filesystem. The Kubernetes API is used for workspace management (`kubernetes://` store URI) and optionally for project execution as Kubernetes Jobs. External LLM provider integrations (OpenAI, AWS Bedrock) are available in the codebase but the AI Gateway feature is disabled by default in RHOAI (`MLFLOW_ENABLE_AI_GATEWAY=false`). [source: Dockerfile.konflux:38-43, 50, mlflow/projects/kubernetes.py:8-9, 32-49, mlflow/gateway/providers/bedrock.py:212, mlflow/deployments/openai/__init__.py:175]\n312:| 1cccfac97 | 2026-08-13 | sync pipelineruns with konflux-central - b977892, triggered_by: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/konflux-central/actions/runs/31667829974 |\n(eval):1: == not found","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3a50adba7ebf18c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
