{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b05b4811d0d9dd4f","type":"tool_result","content":"14:**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of MLflow Tracking Server instances on OpenShift, including Helm-based deployment, Gateway API routing, namespace-scoped workspace RBAC, and integration with the RHOAI platform Auth service. [source: cmd/main.go:171, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:539, internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:67]\n16:**Detailed**: mlflow-operator is a controller-runtime operator that reconciles three custom resources — `MLflow` (mlflow.opendatahub.io/v1), `MLflowOperator` (components.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1), and `MLflowConfig` (mlflow.kubeflow.org/v1) — to deploy and manage MLflow Tracking Server instances on RHOAI. The MLflow controller renders Helm charts into Kubernetes resources (Deployments, Services, PersistentVolumeClaims, CronJobs, NetworkPolicies) and conditionally creates Gateway API HTTPRoutes, OpenShift ConsoleLinks, and Prometheus ServiceMonitors based on cluster capability discovery. A dedicated NamespaceRBACReconciler watches the platform Auth CRD (`services.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1`) and labeled namespaces to propagate view/edit RoleBindings for workspace-level access control. The operator uses leader election, caches filtered by label selectors, and field-selector-scoped watches for shared RBAC objects to minimize API server load. [source: cmd/main.go:171, 272, 300, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:473-552, internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:48-106, internal/controller/routing.go:46-179]\n20:mlflow-operator follows a multi-controller architecture built on controller-runtime v0.23.3. The primary **MLflowReconciler** owns the full lifecycle of MLflow Tracking Server deployments: it renders bundled Helm charts into Kubernetes objects (Deployments, Services, Secrets, ServiceAccounts, PersistentVolumeClaims, CronJobs, and NetworkPolicies) and applies them via server-side apply with owner references. The controller conditionally extends its watch surface at startup using the Kubernetes discovery API — adding Gateway API HTTPRoute, OpenShift ConsoleLink, and Prometheus ServiceMonitor watches only when those CRDs are present in the cluster. This discovery-gated pattern avoids hard dependencies on optional platform APIs. [source: internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:528-552, internal/controller/routing.go:46-179]\n22:The **MLflowOperatorReconciler** manages the component-level `MLflowOperator` CRD from `components.platform.opendatahub.io`, acting as the integration surface for the RHOAI platform operator (odh-operator). A dedicated **NamespaceRBACReconciler** watches namespaces labeled with `opendatahub.io/mlflow-workspace`, the platform Auth CRD (`services.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1`), and MLflow CRs to propagate view/edit RoleBindings into workspace namespaces, enabling multi-tenant access isolation delegated through the platform's centralized auth configuration. [source: internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:48-106, internal/controller/mlflowoperator_controller.go:76-143]\n55:### Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs)\n57:CRD count scope: 3 core API CRDs; 3 total CRD/API rows including configuration and visibility APIs.\n97:| helm.sh/helm/v3 | v3.19.2 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n110:| Gateway API | CRD CRUD | unknown | Manage Gateway API routing resources |\n111:| MLflow (mlflow.opendatahub.io) | CRD Watch | runtime-integration | Read MLflow instances |\n112:| prometheus-operator | CRD CRUD | unknown | Manage Prometheus monitoring resources |\n115:| Auth (services.platform.opendatahub.io) | CRD Watch | runtime-integration | Platform auth configuration for workspace RBAC propagation |\n148:| mlflow-operator-manager-role | gateway.networking.k8s.io | httproutes | create, delete, get, list, patch, update, watch |\n192:| manager-role | gateway.networking.k8s.io | httproutes | create, delete, get, list, patch, update, watch |\n275:- **Control plane reconciliation:** The operator watches MLflow, MLflowOperator, and MLflowConfig CRs, ConfigMaps, and owned resources (Deployments, Services, Jobs, PVCs, Secrets, ServiceAccounts). On reconciliation, the MLflowReconciler renders Helm charts into Kubernetes objects and applies them via the Kubernetes API (port 6443, TLS, ServiceAccount token auth). Owned resources are label-filtered (`app=mlflow`) to minimize watch bandwidth. [source: cmd/main.go:293-318, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:473-552]\n276:- **Conditional platform integration:** At startup, the operator probes the Kubernetes discovery API for Gateway API, ConsoleLink, ServiceMonitor, and MLflowOperator CRDs. When present, additional watches and owned-resource management are activated; when absent, the operator operates without those integrations. [source: internal/controller/routing.go:46-179, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:528-552]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaca424bb73b2f18","agent_id":"adb1d7279032b26ad","session_id":"29d6ac74-9e14-4313-a4f4-972162f9d527","uuid":"cda42e04-0155-4209-8471-4081834c74b7","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:47:26.946Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b05b4811d0d9dd4f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
