ority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) blocks only P0 work; E004 (P1) blocks only P1 work (E005, E006); no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work (validator-confirmed). The P2 bundling into the P0 server epic is tightly coupled (same denial call sites, same request-handling path) and is not a deferrable distinct feature — and the decision is readable directly from the matrix row (`(bundled)` flag with inline rationale), satisfying the bundling-recording check. No priority collapse with deferrable features.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\nDiagram and frontmatter are fully consistent: every edge in the `graph TD` diagram (E001→E002, E002→E003, E004→E005, E003→E006, E005→E006, E004⇢BRANCH-B-E007) has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic, and vice versa; arrows point dependency→dependent per convention. No circular dependencies, no edges to nonexistent epics. Every edge is justified by a construction rule: E001→E002 and E004→E005 are Rule 3 investigation gates (recorded via `gated_by` with `rewrite` actions); E002→E003 is Rule 12 library→consumer (the server's validation delegates to the plugin's workspace resolution, loaded via the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point — a genuine capability dependency, not a convenience edge); E003/E005→E006 are verification ordering. Parallelism is correctly preserved: E001∥E004 and E003∥E005 have no edges, with the E003/E005 coupling correctly expressed as end-to-end ACs rather than a DAG edge (the flag combination the operator renders is a configuration reference, exactly the Rule 13/25 shape the rules mandate). The branch file sets both `branch: B` and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004` (which is in its direct `dependencies`), and no main-plan epic lists the branch epic as a dependency. Critical path E001→E002→E003→E006 (length 4) is reasonable for an M-L strategy with no new components (all three components exist with architecture context). No data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content is missing from the DAG.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nAll work belongs to a single team (MLflow Team), matching the strategy's impacted-teams statement. Epic boundaries follow the strategy's three repositories (plugin: E001/E002; server: E003; operator: E004/E005; cross-component E2E: E006 as one logical verification change, which the strategy's effort estimate itself calls out as a distinct work stream). The canonical-component collapse to `component: MLflow` is correct and transparently documented: `.context/rhai-components.txt` contains exactly one MLflow-related entry, and the summary explains that boundaries follow the strategy's repositories rather than the collapsed Jira label. E005 exceeds the ~2-week guideline (2-3 sprints per the strategy), but this is one logical change over shared Helm chart scaffolding and a single reconciler path — the Health Warnings record all three required elements (estimate, split-would-duplicate rationale, and a concrete conditional re-split trigger by resource kind), so it is a watch item, not a dock. No heterogeneous monoliths, no scope duplication between siblings (E003 implements the server capability; E006 verifies it against the operator-deployed topology — different work products in different environments), no work-product type mixing, no standalone enforcement-tool epic.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nBoth Investigations are genuine: E001's outcome (coupled vs uncoupled) determines whether E002 is verification-only or includes a refactor — a real `rewrite` gate with downstream dependent E002; E004's outcome selects the conditional branch and rewrites E005's routing approach. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (refactored/verified plugin, server capability, operator topology, test suite, delegation configuration). Gating metadata is complete and correct: E002, E005, and BRANCH-B-E007 each populate both `gate_failure_impact.action` and `fallback_approach` with meaningful failure behavior; every epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` sets `gated_by`; every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nSignal fields are type-appropriate throughout: E001/E004 carry `investigation_signals`, the five Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`; no cross-assignment. All seven ai-signals files exist with rationales that match the frontmatter values. Spot-checks against the rubric and `.context`: E004's `local_runnability: 0` is justified (HTTPRoute routing behavior requires a cluster with a gateway implementation, though the selection analysis is desk work); E005's `change_specificity: 0` correctly reflects the undefined CR opt-in surface and pending routing outcome; E006's `open_questions: 0` is right (scenarios are fixed by the strategy's acceptance criteria regardless of routing outcome); E002/E003/E005/E007's `open_questions: -1` values match their gating investigations and strategy risks. `architecture_claims: 1` is properly calibrated: the strategy asserts component states (plugin v1.5.0 registration via `mlflow.workspace_provider`/`WorkspaceStoreRegistry`, server flags in Dockerfile.konflux CMD, operator Helm-in-operator with conditional CRD discovery), and both `mlflow.md` and `mlflow-operator.md` exist in `.context/architecture-context/` and corroborate exactly those premises — the rationales cite them.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable, strategy-derived ACs. E003 carries the strategy's proposed ACs 1-4 verbatim in Given/When/Then form plus the startup-error, stores-uninitialized, no-new-flag, audit-logging, performance-measurement, and S3-scope ACs; E005 carries proposed ACs 5-6 (distinct Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute resources, exact container command flags) plus validation-rejects-misconfiguration, conditional-CRD, and backwards-compatibility ACs. Backwards-compatibility/rollback ACs are present on both E003 and E005 (no konflux chain or docs-authoring epic exists, so those rule-mandated ACs do not apply). No AC states an externally-controlled precondition — the performance AC conditions on measurement of the epic's own work, and the fail-closed AC conditions on behavior the epic itself implements. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by scan of all bodies below frontmatter).\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nFull scope coverage: every strategy capability, proposed acceptance criterion (1-6), risk mitigation (S3 path-resolution scoping, plugin-coupling validation, operator misconfiguration validation, short-TTL caching contingency), assumption, and open question is carried into the epic set or explicitly dispositioned in Health Warnings with rationale (latency question → measurement AC on E003; audit-channel question → bundled server-logs AC with the PM/Security decision recorded as a visible warning; both classify correctly as fix-and-retry scope rather than gating Investigations). The conditional branch covers both bounded outcomes of the routing question (A: no branch epics, routing inside E005; B: BRANCH-B-E007). Documentation Support is listed as \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so no docs-authoring epic is required (Rule 11), and all pending strategy-level process gates are recorded in Health Warnings rather than silently dropped. No strategy-level context is silently omitted.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":42206,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaca424bb73b2f18","agent_id":"adb1d7279032b26ad","session_id":"29d6ac74-9e14-4313-a4f4-972162f9d527","uuid":"d7acc408-97f9-4b0d-bc94-cbfa53be4cfd","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:49:18.351Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
